<!--
  Template for a single skill belonging to a markdown-defined CAP agent.

  Copy this file to srv/<your-agent-folder>/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md (one folder
  per skill), then fill in the placeholders and delete these comments.

  WHAT THIS FILE CONTROLS
  ------------------------
  - The frontmatter `name` and `description` become one entry in the agent's card
    under `skills` — again, metadata for discovery, not a capability gate (see the
    note in AGENTS.md.template: tool availability comes from the CDS model, not
    from whether a skill mentions it).
  - The body becomes instructions injected into the agent's context when this skill
    is relevant — effectively a focused, task-specific addendum to the system prompt
    in AGENTS.md.

  THE NON-OBVIOUS PART: description is routing metadata, not documentation
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  When this agent is itself invoked as a sub-agent/delegation target by a PARENT
  agent (@cap-js/agents auto-connects agent services to each other as delegation
  tools by default), the `description` you write here is what the PARENT model
  reads to decide whether to route a given user request to THIS agent/skill at all.
  It is not read by a human browsing docs — it is read by a model making a routing
  decision, often without ever seeing the rest of this file. Write it accordingly:
  lead with the trigger condition ("the user asks to buy, order, or reserve a
  book"), not with implementation detail. A vague or internally-focused description
  here means a parent agent may simply never delegate to this skill, silently.
-->
---
name: <skill-name>                       # short, stable identifier — used as a key
description: <One sentence: WHEN does this skill apply? Lead with the trigger, not
  the mechanism — a parent agent reads this to decide whether to route here.>
---

# <Skill Display Name>

## When to use

<The situation(s) that should trigger this skill. Be concrete — this restates and
expands the frontmatter description for a human reader, but the frontmatter line is
what a routing model actually sees.>

## Workflow

<Numbered steps the agent should follow once this skill applies, e.g.:
1. Resolve any ambiguous reference (title vs. ID, "some" vs. a specific quantity).
2. Validate preconditions (stock, permissions, whatever applies) before acting.
3. Call the relevant tool/action. Note if it requires human approval
   (@agent.hitl) and that the request will pause.
4. Report the outcome using data returned by the tool, not assumed.>

## Examples

<A few short input → expected-behaviour pairs, including at least one ambiguous
case and how the agent should handle it (e.g. by asking a clarifying question
instead of guessing):
- "<example user request>" → <what the agent does>
- "<ambiguous example>" → <agent asks for clarification instead of guessing>
